WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.337
A basic operation may request abstract software
services such as server work, database

00:00:05.337 --> 00:00:10.985
access and messaging. An overhead matrix maps
each service unit onto CPU instructions, disk

00:00:10.985 --> 00:00:16.571
operations and network messages. Multiplying
requested service units by that matrix yields

00:00:16.571 --> 00:00:22.280
resource quantities, which are then converted
into time using device-specific service costs.

00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:27.520
Carry units through every multiplication to
avoid confusing instructions with thousands of

00:00:27.520 --> 00:00:28.245
instructions.

00:00:28.245 --> 00:00:35.212
The authorization illustration combines 3,110
CPU units at .00001 seconds, fourteen disk

00:00:35.212 --> 00:00:42.417
operations at .02 seconds and one network
operation at .01 seconds. The total no-
contention

00:00:42.417 --> 00:00:49.700
service time is .3211 seconds, approximately .32
seconds. These costs can also become device

00:00:49.700 --> 00:00:52.471
demands for a later queueing model.

00:00:52.471 --> 00:00:57.810
Keep the per-device vector as well as the scalar
sum, because equal total work can create

00:00:57.810 --> 00:01:03.328
very different bottlenecks on different
resources. Why retain separate CPU, disk and
network

00:01:03.328 --> 00:01:08.546
demand after summing execution time? A
contention model needs each resource’s demand to

00:01:08.546 --> 00:01:13.225
identify utilization and bottlenecks; the scalar
total loses that information.

00:01:13.471 --> 00:01:21.328
The final exercise combines validation, one
transaction branch and result delivery. Using

00:01:21.328 --> 00:01:29.008
the displayed matrix, validation costs .07315
seconds, withdrawal .0723, deposit .0986,

00:01:29.008 --> 00:01:36.952
balance inquiry .03715 and result delivery
.01115. The shortest path is .12145 seconds and

00:01:36.952 --> 00:01:42.602
longest .1829 seconds under the given no-
contention assumptions.

00:01:42.602 --> 00:01:47.119
The weighted average additionally depends on
which probability belongs to each branch.

00:01:47.119 --> 00:01:53.800
The printed solution assigns .001 to deposit, .7
to withdrawal and .299 to balance inquiry,

00:01:53.800 --> 00:02:00.482
yielding .14611645 seconds. The extracted
diagram text does not unambiguously establish
the

00:02:00.482 --> 00:02:06.796
edge mapping, so retain that mapping as an
explicit assumption until visually checked.

00:02:06.796 --> 00:02:11.665
Compare the result with alternative
probabilities to show workload sensitivity. End
the

00:02:11.665 --> 00:02:16.814
exercise by identifying which resource costs and
arrival assumptions are still needed before

00:02:16.814 --> 00:02:21.683
claiming performance under concurrent use. Can
we compute the expected path from branch

00:02:21.683 --> 00:02:25.209
times without knowing which probability belongs
to each branch?

00:02:25.296 --> 00:02:30.547
No. A sum of probabilities equal to one is
insufficient; each probability must be paired

00:02:30.547 --> 00:02:31.979
with the correct branch.
